#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079673 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the surface area of a cube whose edges are $3\\,\\textrm{cm}$ in length, like the one shown below. \n\n![](images/e7/e727b20f63c46ef6124d2d33f5db0a003d798f69ec988ee6b41b651e34456387.png)\n\nThe surface area of the cube is the area of all the faces combined. In general, for a cube with sides of length $s,$ there are $6$ sides, each with area $\\mathcal A = s^2.$ So, the surface area is given by\n\n$$\nS = 6\\mathcal A =6 s^2.\n$$\n\nTo find the surface area of the cube above, we substitute $s=3 \\: \\textrm{cm}$ into the formula. This gives\n\n$$\nS = 6(3\\, \\text{cm})^2 = 54 \\,\\textrm{cm}^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the cube is $54\\,\\textrm{cm}^2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c7479702-e5c9-52bf-b435-5bf2abc4badf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4330 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079681 :tutorial/content "The **lateral surface area** of a cube is the surface area of the side faces alone. In other words, it's the surface area that does not include the area of the top or bottom faces.\n\n![](images/78/78fbc4013c4f625f7e5b839ed7545cc6c8a476d31a7f0f5bda8645d73785f70e.png)\n\nThe lateral surface area $S_L$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nS_L = 4s^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of a side." :tutorial/id #uuid "a6bfc760-f618-5908-a6c0-c66506cc9a1d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5401 :tutorial/title "Lateral Surface Areas of Cubes"}]}
